<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>org.pragmatica-lite</groupId>
        <artifactId>pragmatica</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0-rc4</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>script-gate</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>Shell Script Instrument Gate</name>
    <description>
        Test-only module. Owns no production code: it EXECUTES this repository's operator-facing shell
        scripts against constructed conditions and asserts they cannot report success without having
        looked.
    </description>

    <!--
        WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS.

        Two of this repository's scripts reported success while examining nothing. forge.sh ran the
        acceptance suite with `-pl` and no `-am`, so it could pass or fail against installed bytecode
        that was not the tree's (#865). start-postgres.sh printed "PostgreSQL ready" and exited 0 for
        a container that had died 295ms earlier, because its readiness loop had no failure branch
        (#952). Both are the same defect: an instrument whose success carries no information.

        A test that reads a script's TEXT cannot catch this class, because the defect is behavioural -
        the old readiness loop contains the string "PostgreSQL ready" in exactly the position the
        fixed one does. So these tests RUN the real scripts, with the container runtime and Maven
        stubbed on PATH, and assert on the specific diagnostic each one emits. Asserting a non-zero
        exit alone would be worthless here: "it failed correctly" and "the fixture never worked" are
        the same observation, which is why every refusal test is paired with a positive control that
        drives the same fixture to success.

        Running a script needs a process, not a classpath, so this module test-depends on nothing but
        the harness. That is also why it carries no licence header: it links neither the Apache core
        nor the BSL aether modules, unlike cli-docs-gate, which explains its own BSL header by its
        test-scoped dependency on aether/cli. Its position in the root <modules> list is therefore
        free, and it sits BEFORE cli-docs-gate so that module's "LAST, deliberately" stays true.

        JBCT GATING OF THIS MODULE, measured rather than assumed. Test sources are unlinted
        repo-wide (jbct.includeTests defaults to false), so every test-only module - including the
        modules that exist to enforce gates - is unexamined. Setting jbct.skip=false and
        jbct.includeTests=true here was tried and reverted: it produces 19 JBCT-EX-01 errors, one per
        JUnit method, because a test method declares `throws Exception` and the rule requires
        Result<T>. Rewriting a process-and-filesystem harness into Result to satisfy a rule the rest
        of the repository's tests are exempt from would distort the tests without making them
        stronger, so this module inherits the default. That is a real gap and it is written down
        here rather than papered over - claiming a gate this module does not have would be the same
        defect it exists to catch.
    -->

    <properties>
        <!-- Gate-only module: it produces an empty jar and must never be published. -->
        <maven.deploy.skip>true</maven.deploy.skip>
        <gpg.skip>true</gpg.skip>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
            <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
